{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And these words shall be, etc.</b> According to what I explained in the verse ״וְאָהַבְתָּ אֵת ה׳״, it intends to say that one should place the words spoken as a bandage upon his heart when he sees one of these three things (Mo'ed Katan 28a): when he sees a lack of bread for a righteous man, or a lack of life, or a lack of children — he should know that this is not on account of a deficiency in attainment relative to what someone else has attained, but rather that these things are accounted as nothing before Him." "<b>Further</b>, it intends to teach Israel a path whereby the words will be received among them to love HaShem. For love is not an act that a person forces his will to perform the King's commandments; rather it is a thing dependent on the heart, and whenever the heart does not feel something that produces passion within him, he does not love, even if one compels him by every manner of coercion. Therefore the counsel came from El the Counselor, and He said <b>and these words shall be</b> etc. <b>upon your heart</b> — meaning, when he continuously places the words upon his heart, there will be born in his heart a passion of spiritual desire, and his heart will run to the love of HaShem in all that He has commanded him. And this matter — we, orphans of orphans, feel that there is great desire in our hearts and passion for the Elohim of the universe more than all the wealth of the world and the glory of kingship; and everything sweet is felt to be despised and melts in comparison to the tiny portion attained from the feeling of the reality of these words upon our hearts. Happy are we, how good is our portion! And I have already called attention to these matters in several places." "<b>Further</b>, the verse intends three gradations, which comprise the defining boundaries of love: one corresponding to feeling, which is the heart's desire; one corresponding to need, which is the sustenance that gives life to a person; and one in the category of measure — to what extent one must go to discharge the obligation of love. Corresponding to the category of feeling He said <b>with all your heart</b>. Behold, even when a person craves food or wealth, the pleasantness is not so extreme — for although the soul desires to eat, a person can master his spirit to withhold himself from the thing with ease. Not so when the heart's desire enters his heart and he craves a beautiful woman — and all the more so when his passion for her multiplies within his heart, the feeling overwhelms him until he cannot master his spirit to bend his heart, and it forces him to do what he does not wish to do. Go out and learn how much Joseph the righteous exerted himself to subdue the heart's desire in the matter of Potiphar's wife, and his reward will attest to him." "<b>And corresponding</b> to this category the Holy One, blessed be He, said <b>and you shall love HaShem your Elohim with all your heart</b> — that one should strive to bring the love of HaShem into his heart, and inflame himself with His love to the extent of attaining feeling in his heart to whatever degree the heart can feel. Therefore He was precise in saying <b>with all</b>, for the heart desires every woman, and more so one who is beautiful, and even more so one who is the epitome of beauty, and most of all one beyond whom there is none greater. And the verse obligated that the feeling of the heart in love of Him, blessed be He, be in the supreme degree beyond which there is none higher. And this matter is something whose taster knows its essence, and whose taste is pleasant to him beyond measure. And since this category exists by way of arousal, as they said (Numbers Rabbah 10:2) ״the eye sees and the heart desires״ — but without this the heart does not arouse itself to desire — therefore HaShem alerted in a second statement concerning the category of need and said <b>and with all your soul</b>, which are things the soul needs, such as eating and drinking and the like, which a person arouses himself to from himself without the intermediary of anything. So too should the love of HaShem be — and that is His saying <b>with all your soul</b>, that by nature the love of HaShem should be like food and drink. Now these two gradations both exist in the category of that which causes pain and that which intensifies: for food, after eating it and after drinking, the desire for it ceases; and likewise the heart's passion, after the completion of the act in the manner of a man generally (Proverbs 30:19), the matter changes for him until after some time has passed. Therefore the word of HaShem came in a third gradation, in the category of measure, and He said <b>and with all your me'odekha</b> — meaning, with all the wealth that you desire. And just as with wealth the verse said (Ecclesiastes 5:9) ״one who loves silver will not be sated with silver״ — for when a person acquires a great acquisition, then his desire intensifies to desire wealth upon wealth, and his passion goes and grows — so too HaShem commands regarding His love that it not be bounded and limited, and when one degree is reached, the love should grow in it to a degree higher than that. And along this line the measure of His saying <b>me'odekha</b> is not what one has acquired, but what one desires to acquire. And through this there is also resolved what was difficult in this: for if you say it means what you have acquired, then this commandment is not equal for all, since a poor person is not subject to this commandment, and even among men of wealth the commandment would not apply to them equally — unlike the commandments of ״your heart״ and ״your soul״ in which all are equal; and according to our approach it comes out correctly."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {3 "The verse cited from Proverbs 30:19 (דֶּרֶךְ גֶּבֶר בְּעַלְמָה) refers to 'the way of a man with a young woman,' here read literally as consummation of physical desire."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:51.768126523Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}